00:05:57.072 --> 00:05:58.030
MELANIE WARRICK: Great.

00:05:58.030 --> 00:05:58.900
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:05:58.900 --> 00:05:59.830
MELANIE WARRICK: So
Tony, I know you're here

00:05:59.830 --> 00:06:00.940
to talk to us about Bazel.

00:06:00.940 --> 00:06:01.755
What's Bazel?

00:06:01.755 --> 00:06:02.380
TONY AIUTO: OK.

00:06:02.380 --> 00:06:02.880
"Basel."

00:06:02.880 --> 00:06:03.490
"Basil."

00:06:03.490 --> 00:06:04.540
MARK MANDEL: Yeah,
I was going to say,

00:06:04.540 --> 00:06:05.860
I'm Australia-American--

00:06:05.860 --> 00:06:07.240
I'm going to say "Basil," but
you're going to say "Basel."

00:06:07.240 --> 00:06:07.495
MELANIE WARRICK: Oh, I love it.

00:06:07.495 --> 00:06:08.350
"Toh-mah-toh, toh-may-toh."

00:06:08.350 --> 00:06:08.849
OK.

00:06:08.849 --> 00:06:10.300
TONY AIUTO: Right, yes.

00:06:10.300 --> 00:06:14.860
Well, our logo before
the stylized cubes

00:06:14.860 --> 00:06:17.627
was really a basil plant.

00:06:17.627 --> 00:06:18.460
MELANIE WARRICK: Ah.

00:06:18.460 --> 00:06:21.220
TONY AIUTO: And half our team is
in New York, half is in Munich,

00:06:21.220 --> 00:06:22.960
so we get both pronunciations.

00:06:22.960 --> 00:06:24.100
So I'll accept either.

00:06:24.100 --> 00:06:25.020
Everything's good.

00:06:25.020 --> 00:06:28.060
But the origin of it is it
sprung from Google's internal

00:06:28.060 --> 00:06:30.340
build system, which
is called Blaze.

00:06:30.340 --> 00:06:34.180
And "Bazel" is an anagram
of "Blaze," in that sense.

00:06:34.180 --> 00:06:37.720
Blaze started about 10
years ago-- eh, probably 12,

00:06:37.720 --> 00:06:39.280
at this point,
years ago at Google.

00:06:39.280 --> 00:06:42.220
And we've been using it for all
our builds during that time.

00:06:42.220 --> 00:06:45.040
And it's gone through a series
of iterations and rewrites,

00:06:45.040 --> 00:06:49.600
from Python, to Java, and
added this extension language.

00:06:49.600 --> 00:06:51.910
At one point, we decided
to open source it

00:06:51.910 --> 00:06:55.480
and make the Bazel project,
sort of giving back

00:06:55.480 --> 00:06:56.320
to the community.

00:06:56.320 --> 00:07:00.789
And we're finding it's a whole
new bit of work to do that.

00:07:00.789 --> 00:07:02.080
MARK MANDEL: That sounds right.

00:07:02.080 --> 00:07:05.779
So at a high level, what
does Bazel actually do?

00:07:05.779 --> 00:07:06.820
What's the thing it does?

00:07:06.820 --> 00:07:09.010
TONY AIUTO: It is a--
at a very high level,

00:07:09.010 --> 00:07:12.550
it's a build system you can
really make with Autotools,

00:07:12.550 --> 00:07:18.239
or CMake, or Gradle, or Maven,
but places a different focus

00:07:18.239 --> 00:07:19.030
from the ground up.

00:07:19.030 --> 00:07:24.100
In that we want to be able to
build across multiple languages

00:07:24.100 --> 00:07:26.730
and orchestrate
very large builds,

00:07:26.730 --> 00:07:30.550
and run the tests for them
in a very repeatable way.

00:07:30.550 --> 00:07:33.910
Our focus is on fast and
accurate in a pick two.

00:07:33.910 --> 00:07:36.610
We want to do incremental
builds at speed.

00:07:36.610 --> 00:07:38.410
That requires having
a very well-defined

00:07:38.410 --> 00:07:41.920
build so that we know exactly
what to build incrementally.

00:07:41.920 --> 00:07:44.560
And then we can tie
Bazel into systems

00:07:44.560 --> 00:07:48.140
to get remote build execution
and caching of results,

00:07:48.140 --> 00:07:52.570
so that we can really make
a team faster by reducing

00:07:52.570 --> 00:07:54.984
the time it takes
to do their builds.

00:07:54.984 --> 00:07:55.900
MELANIE WARRICK: Cool.

00:07:55.900 --> 00:08:00.340
In terms of Bazel/Basil, what
makes it different, though,

00:08:00.340 --> 00:08:02.440
from other options
that are out there?

00:08:02.440 --> 00:08:05.740
TONY AIUTO: At the bottom,
the key is that all of our

00:08:05.740 --> 00:08:07.540
build steps are hermetic.

00:08:07.540 --> 00:08:09.820
We always know what
goes into something

00:08:09.820 --> 00:08:10.930
and what comes out of it.

00:08:10.930 --> 00:08:12.910
And essentially,
we hash the inputs

00:08:12.910 --> 00:08:16.150
to any rule and the
hash the resultant thing

00:08:16.150 --> 00:08:17.620
so we can cache it very well.

00:08:17.620 --> 00:08:18.910
You've built C applications.

00:08:18.910 --> 00:08:21.160
You know that you
include a .H file,

00:08:21.160 --> 00:08:23.560
and it will get
it from somewhere.

00:08:23.560 --> 00:08:25.330
That doesn't happen
in Bazel builds.

00:08:25.330 --> 00:08:27.790
You always know where you're
getting all the inputs to your

00:08:27.790 --> 00:08:28.570
build.

00:08:28.570 --> 00:08:31.360
So that tactical, strategic
decision at the bottom

00:08:31.360 --> 00:08:34.179
allows us to get
reproducible builds,

00:08:34.179 --> 00:08:37.990
and very precise, incremental,
builds that you don't get,

00:08:37.990 --> 00:08:40.150
let's say, with make-based
things and shell scripts

00:08:40.150 --> 00:08:41.809
gluing stuff together.

00:08:41.809 --> 00:08:44.000
We also [INAUDIBLE] all
the languages at once.

00:08:44.000 --> 00:08:46.330
So we don't have to
change build systems

00:08:46.330 --> 00:08:49.420
in the middle of [INAUDIBLE]
native code for an Android app

00:08:49.420 --> 00:08:52.240
with one set of
tools, and then bundle

00:08:52.240 --> 00:08:55.300
that up and then use, let's say,
Gradle to build the Java parts.

00:08:55.300 --> 00:08:57.531
MARK MANDEL: So how many
languages does it actually

00:08:57.531 --> 00:08:58.030
support?

00:08:58.030 --> 00:08:59.800
TONY AIUTO: That's
a good question.

00:08:59.800 --> 00:09:02.320
There are probably
around 40 rule sets

00:09:02.320 --> 00:09:03.980
that we track right now.

00:09:03.980 --> 00:09:05.980
Some of them are on
the Bazel build site

00:09:05.980 --> 00:09:07.840
and the GitHub project there.

00:09:07.840 --> 00:09:09.910
Others are
independently developed.

00:09:09.910 --> 00:09:14.290
But the big ones are C++,
Java, Python, Go, JavaScript,

00:09:14.290 --> 00:09:17.710
C# and .NET, Scala.

00:09:17.710 --> 00:09:20.950
There's Dart, and Kotlin,
and Swift, and Rust,

00:09:20.950 --> 00:09:24.250
and Ruby too, and others
I haven't even named.

00:09:24.250 --> 00:09:28.220
And orthogonal to that, we
talk about platform support.

00:09:28.220 --> 00:09:31.570
So we want to be able to build
in any of those languages,

00:09:31.570 --> 00:09:34.570
and then deliver to
Docker, or Kubernetes,

00:09:34.570 --> 00:09:37.460
or to different
execution platforms.

00:09:37.460 --> 00:09:40.720
One of the other important
Bazel distinctions

00:09:40.720 --> 00:09:43.300
is that we separate the
notion of the platform you're

00:09:43.300 --> 00:09:46.000
building on from where
you're going to deploy it.

00:09:46.000 --> 00:09:48.670
So we were very clear in
the semantics of, well,

00:09:48.670 --> 00:09:51.670
I happened to be building on
a Windows machine code that's

00:09:51.670 --> 00:09:54.852
going to be deployed on a Linux
server or an Android phone.

00:09:54.852 --> 00:09:55.810
MELANIE WARRICK: Great.

00:09:55.810 --> 00:09:58.290
Well, as a developer,
how do I use Bazel?

00:09:58.290 --> 00:10:00.390
What does it look like
when I'm working with it?

00:10:00.390 --> 00:10:02.170
TONY AIUTO: It's not too
different than other things

00:10:02.170 --> 00:10:02.800
you're used to.

00:10:02.800 --> 00:10:06.430
We have build files, which
are equivalent to a Make file,

00:10:06.430 --> 00:10:10.430
or a Gradle configuration,
or a CMake files.text.

00:10:10.430 --> 00:10:13.660
Inside it are a bunch of
definitions of targets.

00:10:13.660 --> 00:10:16.540
We don't really have executable
code in the build files.

00:10:16.540 --> 00:10:19.230
We want them to be very
declarative, rather than

00:10:19.230 --> 00:10:19.950
procedural.

00:10:19.950 --> 00:10:23.400
So we define targets, and
each one is the name of it,

00:10:23.400 --> 00:10:26.890
the sources that go into it,
and the dependencies it has.

00:10:26.890 --> 00:10:28.440
But from a developer's
point of view,

00:10:28.440 --> 00:10:31.710
they sprinkle the build files
around [INAUDIBLE] their code,

00:10:31.710 --> 00:10:35.190
and will run Builder Blaze
test with a set of targets--

00:10:35.190 --> 00:10:36.410
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:10:36.410 --> 00:10:38.082
TONY AIUTO: --and
the things happened.

00:10:38.082 --> 00:10:40.290
MARK MANDEL: Is it Bazel
build, or is it Blaze build?

00:10:40.290 --> 00:10:41.170
Or you-- you've historically--

00:10:41.170 --> 00:10:42.200
TONY AIUTO: No,
it's a Blaze build.

00:10:42.200 --> 00:10:44.310
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, you have
historical Freudian slips.

00:10:44.310 --> 00:10:46.393
TONY AIUTO: That happens
on the team all the time.

00:10:46.393 --> 00:10:47.660
But it is Bazel build.

00:10:47.660 --> 00:10:49.770
For Blaze build if
you're inside Google.

00:10:49.770 --> 00:10:52.560
The differences between
the products are minimal,

00:10:52.560 --> 00:10:55.580
and we're trying to
eliminate them each day.

00:10:55.580 --> 00:10:57.497
But man, historical
baggage is hard to unload.

00:10:57.497 --> 00:10:59.621
MARK MANDEL: No, that's
really interesting, though.

00:10:59.621 --> 00:11:01.830
So if I'm sitting down
for a new project, am I

00:11:01.830 --> 00:11:05.100
thinking to myself, I'm
building a polyglot project,

00:11:05.100 --> 00:11:07.920
so maybe I will lean
towards Bazel because I

00:11:07.920 --> 00:11:09.240
have multiple languages.

00:11:09.240 --> 00:11:11.370
Is that a contributing
factor to why

00:11:11.370 --> 00:11:12.880
I might choose this as a tool?

00:11:12.880 --> 00:11:14.088
TONY AIUTO: It could be, yes.

00:11:14.088 --> 00:11:16.070
One of the things we
find talking to people

00:11:16.070 --> 00:11:18.170
is you find people
from a community,

00:11:18.170 --> 00:11:19.100
like the Go community.

00:11:19.100 --> 00:11:20.490
The have a wonderful
set of tools

00:11:20.490 --> 00:11:23.550
for building pure Go systems.

00:11:23.550 --> 00:11:25.955
But once you start mixing
it with other languages,

00:11:25.955 --> 00:11:27.330
and you want to
share, let's say,

00:11:27.330 --> 00:11:32.100
a protobuf definition between
your Go and your C++ code,

00:11:32.100 --> 00:11:34.090
then the pure Go
tools fall short.

00:11:34.090 --> 00:11:35.340
I don't mean to pick on Go.

00:11:35.340 --> 00:11:36.870
This is true for
every language that

00:11:36.870 --> 00:11:40.200
has a very well-defined
set of development tools.

00:11:40.200 --> 00:11:43.440
Once you start crossing
over to multilingual things,

00:11:43.440 --> 00:11:45.450
that's where Blaze
really shines.

00:11:45.450 --> 00:11:48.000
Because the single set
of build definitions

00:11:48.000 --> 00:11:50.450
can define your entire product.

00:11:50.450 --> 00:11:53.430
And you don't have to glue
together different systems

00:11:53.430 --> 00:11:55.449
in some sort of shell script.

00:11:55.449 --> 00:11:56.990
MARK MANDEL: Or Make
file, which is--

00:11:56.990 --> 00:11:58.281
TONY AIUTO: Yeah, or Make file.

00:11:58.281 --> 00:12:01.080
Right, the Make file to
call Gradle, to call Make.

00:12:01.080 --> 00:12:03.780
MELANIE WARRICK: Gluing
together is always fun, though.

00:12:03.780 --> 00:12:04.920
We love doing that.

00:12:04.920 --> 00:12:07.290
TONY AIUTO: But it's hard
on your release engineers.

00:12:07.290 --> 00:12:08.886
MELANIE WARRICK: Exactly.

00:12:08.886 --> 00:12:11.149
Release engineers
love that, don't they?

00:12:11.149 --> 00:12:13.440
Well, I know we talked already
a little bit about this,

00:12:13.440 --> 00:12:16.140
but why would we choose this
over something like Make,

00:12:16.140 --> 00:12:18.247
Maven, Gradle, whatever?

00:12:18.247 --> 00:12:19.830
TONY AIUTO: Really,
to reduce the cost

00:12:19.830 --> 00:12:21.090
of your daily development.

00:12:21.090 --> 00:12:23.670
In a nutshell, that's
what the goal is.

00:12:23.670 --> 00:12:25.420
You don't want to
switch to another system

00:12:25.420 --> 00:12:27.240
because it looks nicer.

00:12:27.240 --> 00:12:29.370
You want to do it
because you want

00:12:29.370 --> 00:12:33.060
to make your developer's lives
better by giving them a faster

00:12:33.060 --> 00:12:34.270
turnaround time.

00:12:34.270 --> 00:12:37.650
And we do that through
this fact that each build

00:12:37.650 --> 00:12:39.520
step is a hermetic action.

00:12:39.520 --> 00:12:43.220
So we know that we don't have
to do it again a second time.

00:12:43.220 --> 00:12:45.630
You change one file
in a Bazel build,

00:12:45.630 --> 00:12:48.450
and we will only rebuild
those things that

00:12:48.450 --> 00:12:50.620
absolutely have to get rebuilt.

00:12:50.620 --> 00:12:53.520
We have capability to
hook into caching on a,

00:12:53.520 --> 00:12:57.000
let's say, site-wide basis,
so that all your developers

00:12:57.000 --> 00:13:00.100
at one site can share
each other's results.

00:13:00.100 --> 00:13:03.150
So if you've all pulled down a
common bit of third-party code,

00:13:03.150 --> 00:13:05.760
the first person who builds
it loses for the team.

00:13:05.760 --> 00:13:06.600
They've waited.

00:13:06.600 --> 00:13:09.220
Everyone else gets to
reuse the object module

00:13:09.220 --> 00:13:12.070
that [INAUDIBLE] your teammates
incurred the cost of building.

00:13:12.070 --> 00:13:13.740
So everyone goes
a little faster.

00:13:13.740 --> 00:13:16.230
It's the incrementally,
good, reliable,

00:13:16.230 --> 00:13:19.260
incremental builds
that speed everyone up.

00:13:19.260 --> 00:13:21.060
We do the same thing
on the test side.

00:13:21.060 --> 00:13:22.770
You change one bit,
and instead of having

00:13:22.770 --> 00:13:26.900
to rerun 10,000 tests, we
may only have to rerun 20.

00:13:26.900 --> 00:13:29.640
And you do that for enough
developers enough days,

00:13:29.640 --> 00:13:30.960
and it turns into real money.

00:13:30.960 --> 00:13:31.920
MARK MANDEL: That sounds
really interesting

00:13:31.920 --> 00:13:33.045
from the perspective of it.

00:13:33.045 --> 00:13:35.580
It seems like in today's
development environment,

00:13:35.580 --> 00:13:37.539
you're no longer
pushing out a jar.

00:13:37.539 --> 00:13:39.330
You're no longer just
pushing out a binary.

00:13:39.330 --> 00:13:40.901
You're compiling
some code, and then

00:13:40.901 --> 00:13:42.900
maybe bringing another
library, and putting that

00:13:42.900 --> 00:13:44.858
into a Docker image, and
then taking that image

00:13:44.858 --> 00:13:46.020
and doing stuff with that.

00:13:46.020 --> 00:13:47.436
This sounds like
this sort of tool

00:13:47.436 --> 00:13:51.400
that provides the glue between
the seven different things

00:13:51.400 --> 00:13:52.710
that you need to pull together.

00:13:52.710 --> 00:13:53.730
Would that be accurate?

00:13:53.730 --> 00:13:55.080
TONY AIUTO: Yes, exactly.

00:13:55.080 --> 00:13:57.630
We're not entirely
there yet, but you

00:13:57.630 --> 00:13:59.790
should be able, at one
point, to define a role

00:13:59.790 --> 00:14:02.640
to build a Docker image
and list inside it things

00:14:02.640 --> 00:14:04.890
that were built in
different languages,

00:14:04.890 --> 00:14:07.290
all assembled, multiple
binaries going into it,

00:14:07.290 --> 00:14:09.030
with a single Blaze build--

00:14:09.030 --> 00:14:10.460
Bazel build to target.

00:14:10.460 --> 00:14:12.945
MELANIE WARRICK: (CHUCKLING)
I know, Blaze and Bazel.

00:14:12.945 --> 00:14:14.820
It's easy to go back
and forth between those.

00:14:14.820 --> 00:14:16.415
MARK MANDEL: Exactly, exactly.

00:14:16.415 --> 00:14:18.415
TONY AIUTO: It is tricky
to keep those straight.

00:14:18.415 --> 00:14:19.985
MELANIE WARRICK: But I know
you guys are probably so

00:14:19.985 --> 00:14:20.980
used to it at this point.

00:14:20.980 --> 00:14:22.630
It's pretty common that
you're just like, we get it.

00:14:22.630 --> 00:14:23.050
We get it.

00:14:23.050 --> 00:14:24.008
TONY AIUTO: Well, yeah.

00:14:24.008 --> 00:14:26.160
11 years worth of muscle
memory, and they're

00:14:26.160 --> 00:14:27.210
an anagram of each other.

00:14:27.210 --> 00:14:30.570
And if you're slightly
dyslexic, it's possible.

00:14:30.570 --> 00:14:32.197
MELANIE WARRICK:
So Bazel supports--

00:14:32.197 --> 00:14:34.530
I'm going to just switch
between the two the whole time.

00:14:34.530 --> 00:14:35.576
TONY AIUTO: "Basel" works, yes.

00:14:35.576 --> 00:14:38.040
MELANIE WARRICK: No, I'm kind of
enjoying going "Basel" on one--

00:14:38.040 --> 00:14:38.130
MARK MANDEL: Then "Basil"?

00:14:38.130 --> 00:14:39.420
MELANIE WARRICK: --and
then "Basil" on another.

00:14:39.420 --> 00:14:41.610
So Bazel supports
hermetic execution.

00:14:41.610 --> 00:14:43.700
Does that mean I can
parallelize my work

00:14:43.700 --> 00:14:45.215
load across lots of machines?

00:14:45.215 --> 00:14:46.500
TONY AIUTO: Yes, absolutely.

00:14:46.500 --> 00:14:49.170
Even on a single machine, we
try to parallelize it a bit,

00:14:49.170 --> 00:14:52.810
in that your build builds a
graph of what needs to be done.

00:14:52.810 --> 00:14:56.280
And then after the graph of what
needs to be done is computed,

00:14:56.280 --> 00:14:59.610
we know exactly what execution
steps need to happen.

00:14:59.610 --> 00:15:02.280
And the ones that don't
depend on each other

00:15:02.280 --> 00:15:03.660
can be all done in parallel.

00:15:03.660 --> 00:15:06.630
On a single machine, we
may only spin up for--

00:15:06.630 --> 00:15:08.910
maybe 10 threads worth of
that, because we don't want

00:15:08.910 --> 00:15:10.425
to deadlock your own machine.

00:15:10.425 --> 00:15:12.300
But with a good remote
execution environment,

00:15:12.300 --> 00:15:16.410
we can easily spin up 300,
400 threads of things that

00:15:16.410 --> 00:15:18.450
can be executed in parallel.

00:15:18.450 --> 00:15:20.340
Actually, the remote
build protocol

00:15:20.340 --> 00:15:22.560
is published in a standard.

00:15:22.560 --> 00:15:25.080
We have-- there's some
third parties working

00:15:25.080 --> 00:15:27.250
on things that use this
remote build protocol,

00:15:27.250 --> 00:15:28.860
their own kind of build farms.

00:15:28.860 --> 00:15:32.190
Google has a product
that is alpha testing,

00:15:32.190 --> 00:15:35.640
and it's in-- you can sign up
to try it right now, where we

00:15:35.640 --> 00:15:37.560
provide remote build execution.

00:15:37.560 --> 00:15:40.620
But someone could build their
own remote build server.

00:15:40.620 --> 00:15:43.230
There are some
early Bazel adopters

00:15:43.230 --> 00:15:46.110
who have their own strict
requirements about no code

00:15:46.110 --> 00:15:47.190
off-prem.

00:15:47.190 --> 00:15:49.350
So they want to build
their own remote executors

00:15:49.350 --> 00:15:52.650
so that their code never
has to ship across the wire,

00:15:52.650 --> 00:15:53.915
and they can keep it in-house.

00:15:53.915 --> 00:15:55.290
These were all
possible, and they

00:15:55.290 --> 00:15:58.350
can use Bazel to take
advantage of remote build,

00:15:58.350 --> 00:16:01.080
even without using a
third-party cloud product.

00:16:01.080 --> 00:16:03.286
MARK MANDEL: So I was
doing research on Bazel,

00:16:03.286 --> 00:16:05.910
and we talked a bit before about
all the languages it supports.

00:16:05.910 --> 00:16:06.930
It looks like some
of that support

00:16:06.930 --> 00:16:08.970
comes through extensions,
or there's some kind

00:16:08.970 --> 00:16:10.519
of extension mechanism there.

00:16:10.519 --> 00:16:11.310
How does that work?

00:16:11.310 --> 00:16:13.770
TONY AIUTO: One of the
key differentiators

00:16:13.770 --> 00:16:16.440
between Google's
version of Blaze

00:16:16.440 --> 00:16:19.290
and the new version
of Bazel is that rules

00:16:19.290 --> 00:16:22.800
used to be built into the
Blaze execution, Blaze binary.

00:16:22.800 --> 00:16:25.890
They are all in an
extension language in Bazel,

00:16:25.890 --> 00:16:29.220
and the extension language
is called Starlark now.

00:16:29.220 --> 00:16:34.620
It used to be Skylark, and that
has been renamed for reasons.

00:16:34.620 --> 00:16:36.120
And there's a very
strict separation

00:16:36.120 --> 00:16:39.396
between what a developer
at the high level

00:16:39.396 --> 00:16:41.520
sees in a build file and
the rules that are written

00:16:41.520 --> 00:16:43.320
in this extension language.

00:16:43.320 --> 00:16:45.960
Starlark is Python-esque.

00:16:45.960 --> 00:16:48.480
It looks very much like
Python when you write it,

00:16:48.480 --> 00:16:49.950
with a few restrictions.

00:16:49.950 --> 00:16:52.550
We don't allow
recursion, and since--

00:16:52.550 --> 00:16:55.290
it's got a very similar syntax.

00:16:55.290 --> 00:16:58.500
And at this point, I should
mention one of the things

00:16:58.500 --> 00:17:02.340
that help make Bazel
accurate is its view of what

00:17:02.340 --> 00:17:03.900
we have to build.

00:17:03.900 --> 00:17:06.210
Every command-to-builder
testing is

00:17:06.210 --> 00:17:09.210
done in three phases, where
we load up the things you need

00:17:09.210 --> 00:17:12.119
and all the rules, which is
really just parsing and doing

00:17:12.119 --> 00:17:13.270
things like visibility.

00:17:13.270 --> 00:17:15.900
We can restrict
certain targets in the

00:17:15.900 --> 00:17:19.380
build to a particular
set of other targets,

00:17:19.380 --> 00:17:20.819
so that you can--

00:17:20.819 --> 00:17:25.079
just like in any language, you
would do some object wrapping

00:17:25.079 --> 00:17:27.210
to hide implementation
details, we

00:17:27.210 --> 00:17:30.060
can hide targets from having
everyone in your organization

00:17:30.060 --> 00:17:33.270
exploit the-- or use the things
you don't want to be seen.

00:17:33.270 --> 00:17:35.100
That happens at load time.

00:17:35.100 --> 00:17:37.260
Analysis time is
when we can execute

00:17:37.260 --> 00:17:39.660
these Starlark
rules to determine

00:17:39.660 --> 00:17:41.460
what our build graph should be.

00:17:41.460 --> 00:17:43.860
And we can determine the
delta from the last version

00:17:43.860 --> 00:17:47.280
of the build graph, so we
know what has to be built new.

00:17:47.280 --> 00:17:49.770
And then finally, we get
to an execution phase,

00:17:49.770 --> 00:17:54.870
where we can parallelize like
crazy to do only what we need

00:17:54.870 --> 00:17:56.940
in the shortest time possible.

00:17:56.940 --> 00:18:00.450
And this strict separation
also prevents this loop

00:18:00.450 --> 00:18:02.070
where the results
of an execution

00:18:02.070 --> 00:18:05.550
can feed back into
analysis later on.

00:18:05.550 --> 00:18:08.310
I'm sure everyone listening has
had a project somewhere where

00:18:08.310 --> 00:18:12.062
you run the Make twice in a row
and you get different results,

00:18:12.062 --> 00:18:13.770
because the first time
leaves [INAUDIBLE]

00:18:13.770 --> 00:18:15.980
that the second time picks up.

00:18:15.980 --> 00:18:18.570
And that just doesn't happen
with Bazel-based products.

00:18:18.570 --> 00:18:19.827
MARK MANDEL: So what kind of--

00:18:19.827 --> 00:18:21.660
I mean, we talked about
language extensions.

00:18:21.660 --> 00:18:24.150
But what kind of stuff do
people build with extensions?

00:18:24.150 --> 00:18:25.330
What would people
want to do with that?

00:18:25.330 --> 00:18:27.371
TONY AIUTO: Well, all of
our rule sets right now.

00:18:27.371 --> 00:18:31.050
Well, C++ and Java are still
built into the Bazel core.

00:18:31.050 --> 00:18:31.950
Python a bit too.

00:18:31.950 --> 00:18:33.450
We're moving it out.

00:18:33.450 --> 00:18:35.930
Rules Go is all built in
the extension language.

00:18:35.930 --> 00:18:37.680
The JavaScript, the C# support.

00:18:37.680 --> 00:18:40.050
[INAUDIBLE] is built in
the extension language.

00:18:40.050 --> 00:18:44.640
Really, the Bazel team and
the larger Bazel community,

00:18:44.640 --> 00:18:47.670
and outside companies,
are all contributing

00:18:47.670 --> 00:18:52.230
sets of rules that really make
the Bazel ecosystem bigger

00:18:52.230 --> 00:18:55.394
than the core Bazel
product itself.

00:18:55.394 --> 00:18:57.060
MARK MANDEL: And so
as a company as well

00:18:57.060 --> 00:19:00.300
who has a team of developers, we
could write our own extensions

00:19:00.300 --> 00:19:02.074
that would help us do
specific things that

00:19:02.074 --> 00:19:03.740
are particular to our
companies as well?

00:19:03.740 --> 00:19:03.880
TONY AIUTO: Yeah.

00:19:03.880 --> 00:19:06.210
Well, we can take the
Haskell Rules in particular.

00:19:06.210 --> 00:19:08.880
There's a company
in France, Tweag,

00:19:08.880 --> 00:19:10.800
I hope I'm pronouncing
it right, who

00:19:10.800 --> 00:19:14.700
have done a lovely set of
rules for Haskell, because they

00:19:14.700 --> 00:19:16.860
needed it for their
daily development.

00:19:16.860 --> 00:19:19.530
And they contributed it
back to the outside world.

00:19:19.530 --> 00:19:22.500
So they have other
people using their rules,

00:19:22.500 --> 00:19:25.615
and it requires no modifications
to core Bazel itself.

00:19:25.615 --> 00:19:26.734
MELANIE WARRICK: Great.

00:19:26.734 --> 00:19:28.650
What do you enjoy most
about what you work on?

00:19:28.650 --> 00:19:31.320
TONY AIUTO: Well, I've
been building and selling

00:19:31.320 --> 00:19:32.910
developer tools for years.

00:19:32.910 --> 00:19:36.570
And it's super gratifying
to go into a place

00:19:36.570 --> 00:19:39.160
and have engineers
say, oh, I use this.

00:19:39.160 --> 00:19:41.000
It makes my job easier.

00:19:41.000 --> 00:19:43.380
So that's really what I
love about what I work on.

00:19:43.380 --> 00:19:45.630
And I believe in it too,
because at Google, I've

00:19:45.630 --> 00:19:48.430
shipped products that
were consumer-side.

00:19:48.430 --> 00:19:51.690
And we really care
about the provenance

00:19:51.690 --> 00:19:53.130
of what goes into our code.

00:19:53.130 --> 00:19:56.325
And I think most
product-shipping companies do.

00:19:56.325 --> 00:19:58.260
They have to know
everything that went in,

00:19:58.260 --> 00:20:01.920
and they didn't accidentally
include a GPL code

00:20:01.920 --> 00:20:03.900
in a product that's not
compatible with that.

00:20:03.900 --> 00:20:06.330
Or if they did use an
[INAUDIBLE] library,

00:20:06.330 --> 00:20:09.270
they linked it dynamically
rather than statically.

00:20:09.270 --> 00:20:14.289
And that's really hard to know
with many ad hoc built systems.

00:20:14.289 --> 00:20:16.080
But with Bazel, you
can actually trace down

00:20:16.080 --> 00:20:18.610
the provenance of
everything you've included

00:20:18.610 --> 00:20:21.430
and then script your own
checking against that,

00:20:21.430 --> 00:20:25.012
to make sure that you know
what you are giving to people.

00:20:25.012 --> 00:20:26.470
And that's really
one of the things

00:20:26.470 --> 00:20:29.760
that motivated me to move from
my last team to the Bazel team.

00:20:29.760 --> 00:20:31.510
MARK MANDEL: So what
products are actually

00:20:31.510 --> 00:20:32.630
using Bazel right now?

00:20:32.630 --> 00:20:34.300
I'm guessing there's probably
a lot of Google products

00:20:34.300 --> 00:20:36.370
that probably use Bazel
that are open-source.

00:20:36.370 --> 00:20:39.430
TONY AIUTO: Well, all Google
products, for the most part,

00:20:39.430 --> 00:20:42.370
are built with the internal
version, called the Blaze.

00:20:42.370 --> 00:20:44.830
And most of the
reason for that is

00:20:44.830 --> 00:20:47.770
that internally, we have
some different packaging

00:20:47.770 --> 00:20:48.940
requirements.

00:20:48.940 --> 00:20:51.520
If we were shipping
to a Linux system,

00:20:51.520 --> 00:20:53.860
someone might want a
Debian or RPM package.

00:20:53.860 --> 00:20:57.037
Google has its own thing
that's different from that.

00:20:57.037 --> 00:20:58.120
It's not that it's secret.

00:20:58.120 --> 00:21:00.290
It's just our way
of doing things,

00:21:00.290 --> 00:21:02.800
and there's no benefit in
sharing it with the world.

00:21:02.800 --> 00:21:06.130
No benefit to the rest of the
world if we tried to share it.

00:21:06.130 --> 00:21:10.300
So the Google version of
Blaze is built on Bazel,

00:21:10.300 --> 00:21:11.950
with a few internal additions.

00:21:11.950 --> 00:21:15.160
So really, all Google
products are using Bazel.

00:21:15.160 --> 00:21:18.520
There are-- I can't
list customers directly,

00:21:18.520 --> 00:21:23.080
but I can talk about the recent
BazelCon we had a few weeks ago

00:21:23.080 --> 00:21:23.740
in New York.

00:21:23.740 --> 00:21:24.489
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:21:24.489 --> 00:21:27.460
TONY AIUTO: We did have
nearly 200 external attendees

00:21:27.460 --> 00:21:30.010
representing a hundred
different organizations

00:21:30.010 --> 00:21:34.330
either currently using Bazel,
or evaluating it right now.

00:21:34.330 --> 00:21:39.490
From large multinationals
to small start-ups.

00:21:39.490 --> 00:21:41.950
Some organizations who
are very tight-lipped

00:21:41.950 --> 00:21:43.460
about what they do.

00:21:43.460 --> 00:21:48.250
What I can say is we've found
a real niche in organizations

00:21:48.250 --> 00:21:54.310
that worry a lot about
compliance and re-certifying

00:21:54.310 --> 00:21:55.870
what they're shipping.

00:21:55.870 --> 00:21:59.440
And they love the fact that
they can test extensively

00:21:59.440 --> 00:22:03.040
and accurately, and know
the provenance of everything

00:22:03.040 --> 00:22:03.910
they've gone into.

00:22:03.910 --> 00:22:06.670
So financial
institutions really like

00:22:06.670 --> 00:22:11.200
people doing embedded devices
that go into various consumer

00:22:11.200 --> 00:22:12.580
price products.

00:22:12.580 --> 00:22:14.570
Love it as well for that reason.

00:22:14.570 --> 00:22:16.527
But I should not
mention their names.

00:22:16.527 --> 00:22:17.360
MARK MANDEL: No, no.

00:22:17.360 --> 00:22:17.859
That's--

00:22:17.859 --> 00:22:18.730
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:22:18.730 --> 00:22:19.480
MARK MANDEL: That
was a good answer.

00:22:19.480 --> 00:22:21.313
MELANIE WARRICK: What
are resources and ways

00:22:21.313 --> 00:22:23.260
that developers can
get started with Bazel?

00:22:23.260 --> 00:22:26.130
TONY AIUTO: Going to our
website, Bazel.build,

00:22:26.130 --> 00:22:28.330
and looking at one of
the starter quick starts

00:22:28.330 --> 00:22:29.710
for various languages.

00:22:29.710 --> 00:22:31.930
There is full documentation
of the product.

00:22:31.930 --> 00:22:34.330
I say "product" because
I'm a product guy,

00:22:34.330 --> 00:22:37.090
but of the "project" and
what the features are.

00:22:37.090 --> 00:22:39.490
There is a discussion
group, Bazel Discuss,

00:22:39.490 --> 00:22:40.720
on Google Groups.

00:22:40.720 --> 00:22:43.090
And all the sources
available on GitHub--

00:22:43.090 --> 00:22:45.930
I wouldn't recommend that as the
place to start figuring it out.

00:22:45.930 --> 00:22:48.360
The website is the right place.

00:22:48.360 --> 00:22:52.450
But the GitHub project does
contain many of the rules sets

00:22:52.450 --> 00:22:54.287
that are built by the community.

00:22:54.287 --> 00:22:55.870
So just looking
through that gives you

00:22:55.870 --> 00:22:57.760
an idea of the
kind of support you

00:22:57.760 --> 00:23:00.102
can expect, where you would
to want to adopt Bazel.

00:23:00.102 --> 00:23:01.060
MARK MANDEL: Excellent.

00:23:01.060 --> 00:23:02.470
What cool stuff is
coming in the future?

00:23:02.470 --> 00:23:03.886
What can people
expect from Bazel?

00:23:03.886 --> 00:23:06.920
TONY AIUTO: Well, we're
a 0 point right now.

00:23:06.920 --> 00:23:10.110
We want to get to a
1.0 release in 2019.

00:23:10.110 --> 00:23:12.132
And we haven't announced
a firm date yet.

00:23:12.132 --> 00:23:13.840
MARK MANDEL: I understand
this well, yes.

00:23:13.840 --> 00:23:14.710
Deliberately vague.

00:23:14.710 --> 00:23:16.060
Deliberately vague.

00:23:16.060 --> 00:23:18.640
TONY AIUTO: It's not
that we aren't done,

00:23:18.640 --> 00:23:21.580
that 1.0 is going to
last for five years.

00:23:21.580 --> 00:23:24.190
[INAUDIBLE] come on,
new rules sets develop.

00:23:24.190 --> 00:23:25.665
But the big thing
in ways Bazel 1.0

00:23:25.665 --> 00:23:30.670
that will be a stability promise
and a way to inform users

00:23:30.670 --> 00:23:34.360
well in advance of changes
that might be incompatible

00:23:34.360 --> 00:23:37.900
and breaking, so that they
have a long time to migrate,

00:23:37.900 --> 00:23:40.210
and it's very
easy, conceptually,

00:23:40.210 --> 00:23:43.030
to figure out how to
migrate to newer versions.

00:23:43.030 --> 00:23:46.360
We also want to bring the
rule-owners into that.

00:23:46.360 --> 00:23:49.540
Because when we have Scala
rules, depending on the Java

00:23:49.540 --> 00:23:52.930
rules, and they each
depend on Bazel,

00:23:52.930 --> 00:23:57.430
it does the community no good if
the Scala rules and Java rules

00:23:57.430 --> 00:24:00.640
each are built to depend on
a different version of Bazel.

00:24:00.640 --> 00:24:05.350
So we want to get the community
behind phased releases,

00:24:05.350 --> 00:24:08.830
so that end users don't
have any surprises.

00:24:08.830 --> 00:24:10.930
And that's the big
guarantee for 1.0.

00:24:10.930 --> 00:24:13.660
The rest of it is more
usable documentation.

00:24:13.660 --> 00:24:16.900
Removing all nits that were
built in Google long ago,

00:24:16.900 --> 00:24:19.772
and have been carried further
and we should have killed off.

00:24:19.772 --> 00:24:21.730
So we're trying to get
those out of the project

00:24:21.730 --> 00:24:23.690
now while we still can.

00:24:23.690 --> 00:24:24.740
And then more rules.

00:24:24.740 --> 00:24:26.950
That's more language support.

00:24:26.950 --> 00:24:28.740
That's the short-term
future for Bazel.

00:24:28.740 --> 00:24:29.490
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:24:29.490 --> 00:24:30.460
